<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1862 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">State Anthem of Ukraine</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="uk">Державний гімн України</bdi> / <bdi lang="uk-Latn">Derzhavnyi himn Ukrainy (original title: Shche ne vmerla Ukraina)</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A poem written in Kyiv in 1862 and set to music by a priest in 1863; it became the national anthem of Ukraine in 1992.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Pavlo Chubynskyi</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Mykhailo Verbytskyi</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Ukrainians; the Ukrainian national movement</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Kyiv (words) and Western Ukraine (music), Ukraine</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words autumn 1862; music 1863; first choral performance 1864</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Pavlo Chubynskyi (1862); music by Mykhailo Verbytskyi (1863))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>In the autumn of 1862, at a gathering of Serbian and Ukrainian students in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> apartment, the ethnographer Pavlo Chubynskyi heard Serbian students sing Svetozar Miletić's &quot;Srpska pesma&quot; and, according to a memoirist who was there, wrote his poem on the spot. Scholars think it was also influenced by the Polish patriotic song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a>&quot; (1797), popular among the peoples of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> then struggling for independence; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/january-uprising">January Uprising</a> began a few months later. The earliest Ukrainophiles took up the words at once, and in 1862 the head of the gendarmes, Prince Vasily Dolgorukov, exiled Chubynskyi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Arkhangelsk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> for his &quot;dangerous influence on the minds of commoners&quot;.</p><p>The poem was first published in 1863 in the fourth issue of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> journal Meta, which wrongly attributed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taras-shevchenko">Taras Shevchenko</a>. It became popular in what is now Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and Mykhailo Verbytskyi, a priest of the Ukrainian Greek Catholic Church and a prominent composer, set it to music in 1863. The first public choral performance was at the Ruska Besida <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in Lviv in 1864, and the words were first printed with his music in 1865. That year it was sung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl">Przemyśl</a>, then in the Austrian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>, at a commemoration of Shevchenko; this performance is the origin of Ukraine's National Anthem Day, held each year on 10 March. Mykhailo Zazuliak, an emigrant from Lviv living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, performed it as a folk song in 1915, and Columbia issued one of its first recordings in 1916.</p><p>In the first half of the 20th century, during attempts to found a Ukrainian state from lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, the song served as the anthem of the Ukrainian People's Republic from 1917 (though other anthems were also used until 1921), of the West Ukrainian People's Republic, and in 1939 of Carpatho-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. After the Ukrainian SSR joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1922 the song was banned, and it was rejected as a republican anthem in order to suppress separatist feeling. Between 1944 and 1949 Soviet authorities had a new anthem of the Ukrainian SSR written to a prescribed outline, with music by Anton Lebedynets and words by Pavlo Tychyna and Mykola Bazhan; it was adopted on 21 November 1949.</p><p>The song was sung as the de facto anthem at the inauguration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-kravchuk">Leonid Kravchuk</a>, the first president of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, on 5 December 1991. Parliament adopted it as the state anthem on 15 January 1992, and the Constitution of 28 June 1996 named Verbytskyi's music. The words followed only on 6 March 2003, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verkhovna-rada">Verkhovna Rada</a> passed the Law on the State Anthem proposed by President Leonid Kuchma, by 334 votes of 450: the first stanza and refrain of Chubynskyi's poem, with the first line slightly changed.</p><p>The anthem became especially popular after the Orange Revolution of 2004 and the Euromaidan protests of 2013, when protesters, led by the singer Ruslana, sang it once an hour in the early weeks; Nadia Diuk called it the clarion call of that revolution. The composer Valentyn Sylvestrov heard in it the beginning of a church liturgy, a hallelujah. After the Russian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 2022 several orchestras in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> played it in solidarity with Ukraine.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic song declaring that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, its glory and its freedom live on, and calling its people to stand together for their freedom. Solemn and hymn-like rather than martial.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Ukrainian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Ukrainian</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Ще не вмерла Украіна,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Šče ne vmerla Ukraina,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ukraine has not yet died,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">И слава, и воля!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Y slava, y volja!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Nor her glory, nor her freedom!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Ще намъ, браття-молодці,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Šče namʺ, brattja-molodci,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Still upon us, brothers, brave lads,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Усміхнетця доля!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Usmixnetcja dolja!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fate will smile!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Згинуть наші вороги,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Zhynutʹ naši vorohy,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our enemies will perish</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Якъ роса на сонці;</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Jakʺ rosa na sonci;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Like dew in the sun;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Запануємъ, браття, й ми</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Zapanujemʺ, brattja, j my</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We too, brothers, shall be masters</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">У своій сторонці.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">U svoij storonci.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In our own land.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Душу, тіло ми положимъ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Dušu, tilo my položymʺ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Soul and body we shall lay down</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">За свою свободу</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Za svoju svobodu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For our freedom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">И покажемъ, що ми браття</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Y pokažemʺ, ščo my brattja</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And we shall show that we, brothers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Козацького роду.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Kozacʹkoho rodu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Are of Cossack kin.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Гей-гей, браття миле,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Hej-hej, brattja myle,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hey, hey, dear brothers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Нумо братися за діло!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Numo bratysja za dilo!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, let us set to the work!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Гей-гей пора встати,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Hej-hej pora vstaty,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hey, hey, it is time to rise,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Пора волю добувати!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Pora volju dobuvaty!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Time to win freedom!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Наливайко, Залізнякъ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Nalyvajko, Zaliznjakʺ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Nalyvaiko, Zalizniak</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">И Тарасъ Трясило</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Y Tarasʺ Trjasylo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And Taras Triasylo</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Кличуть насъ изъ-за могилъ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Klyčutʹ nasʺ yzʺ-za mohylʺ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Call us from beyond the graves</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">На святеє діло.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Na svjateje dilo.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To the holy work.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Изгадаймо славну смерть</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Yzhadajmo slavnu smertʹ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us remember the glorious death</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Лицарства-козацтва,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Lycarstva-kozactva,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of the Cossack knighthood,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Щобъ не втратить марне намъ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Ščobʺ ne vtratytʹ marne namʺ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So that we do not lose in vain</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Своєго юнацтва.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Svojeho junactva.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our youth.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Ой Богдане, Богдане,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Oj Bohdane, Bohdane,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Oh Bohdan, Bohdan,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Славний нашъ гетьмане!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Slavnyj našʺ hetʹmane!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our glorious hetman!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">На-що віддавъ Украіну</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Na-ščo viddavʺ Ukrainu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Why did you give Ukraine away</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Москалямъ поганимъ?!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Moskaljamʺ pohanymʺ?!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To the vile Muscovites?!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Щобъ вернути іі честь,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Ščobʺ vernuty ii čestʹ,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To win back her honour</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Ляжемъ головами,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Ljažemʺ holovamy,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall lay down our heads,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Назовемся Украіни</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Nazovemsja Ukrainy</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall call ourselves Ukraine's</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Вірними синами!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Virnymy synamy!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Faithful sons!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Наші браття Славяне</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Naši brattja Slavjane</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our brothers the Slavs</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Вже за зброю взялись;</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Vže za zbroju vzjalysʹ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Have already taken up arms;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Не діжде ніхто, щобъ ми</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Ne dižde nixto, ščobʺ my</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>No one shall live to see us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">По-заду зістались.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Po-zadu zistalysʹ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Left behind.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Поєднаймось разомъ всі,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Pojednajmosʹ razomʺ vsi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us all unite together,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Братчики-Славяне:</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Bratčyky-Slavjane:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Brother Slavs:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Нехай гинуть вороги,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Nexaj hynutʹ vorohy,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let the enemies perish,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Най воля настане!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Naj volja nastane!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let freedom come!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Pavlo Chubynskyi; the first printing, in the Lviv journal Meta (1863), in its spelling. Text from <a href="https://uk.wikisource.org/wiki/%D0%9C%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%B0%2F1863%2F4%2F%D0%A9%D0%B5_%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%B2%D0%BC%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BB%D0%B0_%D0%A3%D0%BA%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%96%D0%BD%D0%B0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (scientific transliteration).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: the letters as printed in 1863 (і where modern spelling has ї, final ъ written ʺ, ь written ʹ, х written x).</li><li>line 1.2: literally 'And glory, and freedom!': neither the glory nor the freedom of Ukraine has died.</li><li>line 1.3: 'браття-молодці' is 'brothers, fine young fellows', a usual address in Cossack songs.</li><li>line 1.7: 'запануємо' is 'we shall rule, be our own masters'.</li><li>line 4.1-4.2: Severyn Nalyvaiko (d. 1597), Maksym Zalizniak (18th century) and Taras Fedorovych, called Triasylo (17th century), led Cossack and peasant risings.</li><li>line 4.6: 'Лицарства-козацтва', literally 'of the knighthood, of the Cossackdom'.</li><li>line 5.1: Bohdan Khmelnytsky, hetman, who in 1654 placed the Hetmanate under the Tsar of Muscovy.</li><li>line 5.4: 'москалі' is a disparaging name for Russians (Muscovites); 'погані' means bad, vile, also heathen.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
